Carbon dioxide sensors based on NDIR technology need to be calibrated before using. Calibration is to measure and test the sensor, and to establish the corresponding relationship or model between the sensor test voltage value and CO2 concentration. However, in addition to the influence of CO2 concentration itself on the voltage value, factors such as ambient temperature, pressure, sensor manufacturing technology, and light source stability also have a certain impact on the output voltage value of the sensor. The measured concentration output of the sensor may be discrepant with the actual concentration. Therefore, accurate calibration of carbon dioxide sensor can provide the basic guarantee for accurate measurement of CO2 concentration. Carbon dioxide sensors for accurate calibration need to spend a lot of manpower, financial resources and time cost. Therefore, to achieve a highly efficient, accurate and low-cost calibration method has a good application value and market prospects.
