Non-dispersive infrared carbon dioxide sensors can be divided into single-channel and dual-channel. The cost of single-channel carbon dioxide sensors cost is less than dual-channel carbon dioxide sensors. In this paper, based on NDIR technology and the idea that there are commonalities among different gas sensors, we proposed an efficient, lowcost and highly reliable fast calibration algorithm for single-channel carbon dioxide sensors. In the same material and formation process, the data model established by each gas sensor also has some similarities. We have established a certain number of relevant data models, it can estimate the data model of the newly produced sensor through the relevant data models and the measurement data of the newly produced sensor, so as to realize the sensor calibration of the newly produced sensor. The main contributions of this paper are as follows: (1) This paper proposes a fast calibration algorithm based on deep learning. The algorithm uses the existing model and a small number of data points to build a new sensor model, thus saving a lot of time of measuring data. It not only saves various costs, but also has higher efficiency and accuracy. (2) According to industry standards, our algorithm can achieve a data point qualified rate of 91.542% at 0 ◦C–45 ◦C and a data point qualified rate of 99.063% at 20 ◦C–35 ◦C. Compared with the two competing products, the average qualified rate of the algorithm data points have increased by 12.315% and 22.732% respectively. The rest of this paper is structured as follows. Section 2 reviews the related work of carbon dioxide sensors based on NDIR. Section 3 describes the relevant principles involved in the algorithm. Section 4 describes the three type of model used in the algorithm. Section 5 presents the specific design of the algorithm. In Section 6, the performance of the proposed method is experimentally evaluated, and Section 7 concludes this paper.
2. Related work
As technology matures and commercialization develops, most products are moving towards low-cost development. In the literature [12, 13], in order to reduce the cost of CO2 monitoring in the environment, a dual-channel sensor is adopted and the sensor is optimized. Although the optimization results were significant, the cost of the sensors themselves did not decrease. Part of the reason for good optimization is that dual-channel sensors have been able to eliminate some of the environmental impacts to a certain extent. Compared with the dual-channel sensor, the single-channel sensor has a lower cost. In terms of calibration of carbon dioxide sensor, multiple linear regression method was used in the literature [12,14,15], random forest model was used in the literature [16], and artificial neural network model was used in the literature [15]. The existing literature mainly aims to obtain sensor models with better performance, and most of the literature only optimizes the dual-channel sensor to replace the gas monitor. The cost of the sensors by these methods is much cheaper than gas monitors, and it can increase the range of monitoring by these methods, but it is much more expensive than single-channel carbon dioxide sensors. In the literature [17,18], the neural network model was used to calibrate the carbon dioxide sensor. But when building a sensor model for the new sensor, it needs to collect 40 points, and each data point needs to take 30 to 40 min. It takes about 24 h for modeling the sensor, and it require a large amount of gas for 40 data points. However, the algorithm designed by us only needs to collect six data points for the newly produced sensor. Hence, our algorithm dramatically reduces the financial cost and time cost.
