3. Principle of infrared gas sensor
The simple structure diagram of a single-channel infrared gas sensor is shown in Fig. 1, which mainly includes an infrared light source, filter, sensitive component, gas inlets and outlets. Most gases have a specific absorption wavelength in the nondispersive infrared wavelength range, and the absorption peak of CO2 is located at 4.26 μm [19,20]. The absorption of infrared radiation by CO2 satisfies the Lambert–Beer law [21]:
I = I0exp(−kCL) (1)
Among them, I0 is the intensity of incident light, I is the intensity of emitted light, k is the absorption coefficient of CO2, L is the effective optical length of CO2 absorption, and C is the concentration of CO2. Theoretically, the effective wavelength of light absorbed by CO2 in the sensor is a fixed value. In the same environmental conditions, the absorption coefficient k is basically a fixed value, and we just need to know the incident light intensity I0 and emission intensity I, then it can calculate the concentration C of CO2. However, it is influenced by some factors such as temperature and pressure in a real environment, absorption coefficient k is very complex and changeable, so it is difficult to maintain a constant state. If it directly use Eq. (1), it is very difficult to obtain the concentration. To a certain extent, it can reduce the influence of environmental factors with a dual-channel carbon dioxide sensor, but the manufacturing cost of a dual-channel sensor is much higher than a single-channel sensor. Therefore, we need to take measures to eliminate the impact of these environmental factors at a low cost.
