4.1. BP neural network
BP neural network is a feedforward neural network based on BP algorithm [22]. It includes input layer, hidden layer, and output layer, which is as shown in Fig. 2. BP neural network can learn and store a large number of mapping relations between input and output models, and it does not need to know the mathematical equations of these mapping relations in advance [23,24]. The original output voltage and temperature in the cavity are used as input values, and the gas distribution parameters of the experimental equipment are used as label values to train the BP neural network model. It can eliminate the influence of environmental factors to a certain extent, and make the measured value closer to the actual value. And in terms of the complexity of the sensor model, the BP neural network is sufficient, which will be explained in subsequent experiments. The BP neural network used in this paper is a three-layer network model. There is an input layer, a hidden layer, and an output layer. The sensor output voltage (AD value) and the sensor cavity temperature (T) are used as input values. The hidden layer uses 7 neurons. The output value is the concentration value (C) of CO2. The hyperbolic tangent function is used as the activation function between the input layer and the hidden layer. The sigmoid function is used as the activation function between the hidden layer and the output layer.
4.2. LSTM neural network
LSTM (Long short-term memory) is a special Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) [25]. The neural network structure of LSTM is the same as that of ordinary RNN, and its structure is shown in Fig. 3. RNN allows the information of the previous step to be passed to the next step. But as the number of cycles increases, RNN cannot effectively use historical information. To solve this problem, LSTM was born. Its main function is to solve the problem of gradient disappearance and gradient explosion during long sequence training. Compared with RNN, LSTM can perform better in longer sequences. In this problem, the measurement of carbon dioxide concentration is a long-term continuous process. The use of LSTM can save memory information during the process of using and reduce the impact of environmental factors. The specific advantages and disadvantages need to be concluded in the experimental process. The main difference between LSTM and RNN is the structure of block A. The main structure of block A is shown in Fig. 4. It mainly includes three gates (forget gate, input gate, output gate) [26,27].
