4.3. GRU neural network
GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit) is simplified from LSTM [28,29]. GRU is a variant of LSTM, which is with a simpler structure than LSTM, and has more advantages when building large models. But in this problem, whether GRU has a better effect than LSTM needs to be verified through experiments. Its structure is shown in Fig. 5. zt stands for update gate, and its function is similar to the forget gate and input gate of LSTM. It determines what information is discarded and what new information is added. rt stands for the reset gate, and the reset gate is used to determine the degree to which the previous information is discarded. ̃Yt is the output gate, which is similar to the role of LSTM. The specific calculation process is shown in the Eq. (7).
zt = σ(Wzy ∙ Yt−1 + Wzx ∙ Xt)
rt = σ(Wry ∙ Yt−1 + Wrx ∙ Xt)
̃Yt = tanh(W ∙ [rt ∙ Yt−1, Xt])
Yt = (1 − zt) ∙ Yt−1 + zt ∙ ̃Yt
(7)
The calculation method of Eq. (7) is similar to that of LSTM. Yt is calculated differently. When calculating Yt, a ‘‘1-’’ operation is performed on the calculated zt, and the value of zt is subtracted from 1 and then multiplied by Yt−1.
5. Algorithm
The main idea of the algorithm is to match multiple adapted general models with the six data points measured by the new sensor, and establish a new sensor model through the adapted general model. In this way, a new model can be established after measuring only six data points for a newly produced sensor. Compared with normal calibration, the required measurement time and measurement gas are greatly reduced, so the calibration efficiency is improved and the cost is reduced. And because of the characteristics of the neural network, the new model established has high accuracy at the same time. The types of general models mainly include BP neural network, LSTM neural network and GRU neural network. The difference among the three models is mainly in the types of neurons used in the hidden layer. A type of neural networks model with the best comprehensive performance is selected as the general model using model through experimental comparison in the experimental part. The main steps of the fast calibration algorithm are as follows:
• Step 1: N uncalibrated sensors are taken to measure group m data points, namely AD value at a certain temperature T and a certain concentration C. (T, AD, C) is taken as a group of data points. The N is larger, the number of model samples will be more. The m is larger, the precision of a single model is higher. Obviously, the algorithm works better when N and m are larger. However, an increase in N and m will greatly enhance the time and financial cost of data collection. • Step 2: The sequence of m data points collected by each sensor is randomly disrupted, and normalization is carried out. The specific normalization method is shown in the following section. • Step 3: For each sensor, 20% of the data points are randomly selected as a validation set training model using neural network model training. And N neural network models are obtained. • Step 4: For the newly produced sensor to be calibrated, AD values at the two concentration points of 400 ppm and 2000 ppm are measured at each temperature point (i.e. 15 ◦C, 25 ◦C, 35 ◦C). • Step 5: We perform the following operations on N neural network models generated in Step 2: firstly, we normalize the six data points generated in Step 3, and then substitute N neural network models for calculation (the normalization method is shown in the next section). The output value of the model is obtained by calculation. CO2 concentration is acquired by the inverse normalization of the output value. The population standard deviation of the difference between the six predicted values and the true values is gained by calculation. • Step 6: We select multiple from N model overall model as models of the minimum standard deviation estimation model. The output values of these models are calculated and the average value of these output values is taken as the true output value.
6. Experiment and result analysis
6.1. Data collection
In the early data collection stage, we used air and carbon dioxide to match the carbon dioxide concentration value in the gas, but the measured data had errors, which led to certain deviations in data analysis and model building. Later, we used pure CO2 gas and pure N2 gas to carry out gas distribution and data acquisition in a professional temperature box. We were using a high-performance new production of carbon dioxide sensor as the test object. We have collected forty points, temperature from 0 ◦C to 45 ◦C, it is a temperature point for every 5 ◦C, and there is a total of ten temperature points; Data of four concentration points (i.e. 400ppm, 1000ppm, 2000ppm, 4000ppm) were collected at each temperature point. The specific steps of collection are as follows:
• Step 1: We put the sensor into the corresponding temperature box and set the target temperature in the calibration system. The temperature in the temperature box will be constant in about an hour. • Step 2: According to the target concentration value, gas distribution is carried out in the temperature box. After 30–40 min, the gas concentration stabilizes, and a measurement operation is completed. • Step 3: Step 2 is repeated until the AD value of the sensor at all concentration values is recorded at the specified temperature point. • Step 4: The procedures of Steps 1 through 3 are repeated until the sensor AD values at each concentration at all temperature values are recorded at regular intervals of 5 ◦C between 0 ◦C and 45 ◦C. • Step 5: We process the obtained experimental data and acquire eight thousand data points from it as the data set for model training and testing. The specific process is as follows.
