6.2. Data normalization
The sensor is designed to have a temperature range of 0 ◦C to 45 ◦C and a CO2 concentration range of 400ppm to 4000ppm. Firstly, the selected six data points only contain three temperatures (i.e. 15 ◦C, 25 ◦C, 35 ◦C) and two concentrations (i.e. 400ppm, 2000 ppm). Secondly, it is impossible to obtain a large number of data points at different temperatures and concentrations at one time during actual use. Therefore, during calibration, we do not know the maximum and minimum of the actual value of the new sensor. So, during normalization, only six data points can be used to predict the theoretical maximum and minimum values. For the temperature in the sensor cavity, we normalize it by the following method. We obtain the temperature in the cavity when the ambient temperature is 35, and add 10 to this temperature, and the obtained value is the estimated value of the maximum temperature in the sensor cavity. Similarly, we obtain the temperature in the cavity when the ambient temperature is 15, and minus the temperature by 15, and the obtained value is the estimated value of the minimum temperature in the sensor cavity. For AD value, we normalize it by the following method. We first obtain the AD value when the ambient temperature is 35 and the concentration is 400 ppm. And then we are going to add 500 to this value, which is the maximum estimated value of AD value. Similarly, we first get the AD value of the data point when the ambient temperature is 15 and the concentration is 2000 ppm. And then we subtract 2500 from this value, which is the minimum estimated value of AD value. The difference between the AD value at 35 ◦C and 400 ppm and the actual maximum AD value of each sensor is calculated. Then, the statistic of 500 is obtained by selecting the maximum of the difference and increasing the maximum appropriately. Similarly, the difference between the AD value at 15 ◦C and 2000 ppm and the actual minimum AD value of each sensor is calculated. Then, the statistic of 2500 is obtained by selecting the maximum of the difference and increasing
the maximum appropriately. We draw some conclusions from the feasibility analysis experiment in the early stage. Due to the different characteristics of different sensors, if the theoretical maximum value is used for normalization, it may lead to comparatively large differences in the general model, which is not conducive to the establishment of the general model. When the new sensor is normalizing, the actual maximum and minimum values may exceed the theoretical maximum and minimum values. We limit the actual maximum and minimum values to the theoretical maximum and minimum values, because the exceeded value will not actually be too large. For label value, we use 4000 ppm as the estimated maximum value and 0 ppm as the estimated minimum value.
6.3. Model building and selecting
We randomly select 140 sensor data sets to ensure the randomness of model samples. The normalized input value and label value are used to train the neural network models. After 2000 iterations, the neural network model of the sensor is obtained, and the model is saved to use later. The data set of the remaining 60 sensors are used as the test data set. We select six data points from the data set including three temperature points (i.e. 15 ◦C, 25 ◦C, 35 ◦C) and two concentrations (i.e. 400 ppm, 2000 ppm). These data points are reference data points for selecting the model. The main purpose of choosing these six data points is to save costs. Because increasing or decreasing the temperature and increasing the concentration will increase the cost. And in actual use, the focus is on the accuracy of carbon dioxide concentration measurement at room temperature. The above data points are selected after comprehensive consideration. For model selection, firstly, the normalized six data points are used as the input value of the model, and the output value of the model is obtained. Secondly, the output values are denormalized to obtain the predicted value of CO2 concentration. Then, the population standard deviation between the six predicted values and the true value are obtained by calculating. Finally, multiple models are chosen with the smallest population standard deviation and these models are the estimated model of the new sensor. The average of these output values is the true output value. Using the average of these models as the output value can reduce the individual impact of a single model, and make the established model closer to the actual model of the sensor to be calibrated. This paper uses three neural network models (i.e. BP, LSTM, GRU) to compare the results. The comparison results are shown in Fig. 6, Fig. 7, and Fig. 8. According to Fig. 6, comparing the data point qualified rates of the three models, the average data point qualified rates of the three models are similar, and they are all around 90% in the range of 5–10 hidden layer nodes. According to Fig. 7, comparing the time required to establish a model of the three models, the average time consumed by a single model of the BP neural network is 2.197 s, which is much lower than the other two models. According to Fig. 8, comparing the time consumption of the model selection and the qualified rate verification of the three models, the average time consumed by a single sensor of the BP neural network is 18.402 s, which is less than the other two models. According to the theory, BP neural network is the simplest neural network model among the three neural networks. Although the structure of the BP neural network is simple, its effect can meet the requirements of this paper. After experimental comparison, not only its data point qualified rate is slightly higher than the other two models, but also its time consumption is lower than the other two models, and it has higher accuracy and efficiency. Therefore, we finally decide to use BP neural network as the neural network model used in this paper after experimental comparison. According to the experimental results, the number of hidden layer neurons has little effect on the time consumed for model establishment, model matching, and qualified rate verification in the range of 5–10 hidden layer nodes. Finally, the number of hidden layer nodes is selected as 7 according to the data point qualified rate.
