6.4. Results
For the same number of test sensors, the more the number of reference models we have, the better the effect of model building we get. Because the more the number of reference models, the more probability of the existence of suitable models. Therefore, the algorithm will have a higher data point qualified rate and a better effect. The experimental grouping is shown in Table 2 and the result is shown in Fig. 9. Fig. 9 shows that Group 5 has the highest data point qualified rate compared with other groups and the method of selecting multiple models. And selecting multiple models to establish a new model has significantly better effects compared to selecting a single model as the model of the new sensor. It means that this method can eliminate the individual characteristics of a single model to some extent, and make the established model closer to the actual model of the new sensor. And the effect of the algorithm will be better if the number of approximate models selected increases. But there is also an upper limit, and the time cost will be more, so the number of approximate models selected cannot be increased without a limit. The qualified rate of data points basically reaches the peak in the case of using Group 5 and selecting three approximate models at the same time. And choosing more models does not have a relatively large improvement. Therefore, it finally sets the number of the selecting models to three in our paper.
