6.5. Comparison with competing products
This paper compares the single-channel carbon dioxide sensors of other companies with our algorithm. Two competing products are selected for this experiment. 48 sensors of each competing product are selected, and a total of nine data points were measured at three temperature points (i.e. 5 ◦C, 20 ◦C and 35 ◦C) and three concentration points (i.e. 400 ppm, 2000 ppm, 4000 ppm) for the sensor. The result is shown in Table 5. Based on the above calculation of the qualified rate, the average qualified rate of the data points of competitive Product 1 is 69.907%. And the average qualified rate of the data points of the competitive Product 2 is 80.324%. It can be seen from the data in Table 5 that the effect of Product 1 at higher temperatures is extremely poor. At lower temperatures, the effect of Product 1 is similar to our algorithm. In other cases, it has a big gap with our algorithm. Product 2 is extremely poor at lower temperatures. The effect of Product 2 is similar to our algorithm at higher temperatures. In other cases, it has a big gap with our algorithm. As shown in Table 5, our algorithm increases the qualified rate of data points relative to Product 1 by 22.732% and increases the
qualified rate of data points relative to Product 2 by 12.315%. And our algorithm is more stable and has better results at various temperatures compared with these two competing products. According to Table 4, our algorithm has an excellent effect between 10 ◦C and 40 ◦C, and the data point qualified rate reaches 97.738%. The data point qualified rate even reaches 99.063% when the temperature is between 20 ◦C and 35 ◦C.
7. Conclusion
In order to save costs while improving calibration efficiency and accuracy, we proposed a fast calibration algorithm for Non-Dispersive Infrared(NDIR) single-channel carbon dioxide sensor based on deep learning. Firstly, we establish N network models which consist of N sensors by collecting m data points from different temperatures and concentrations. Secondly, we collect six data points from a new sensor which are measured at three temperatures and two concentrations. Thirdly, we choose multiple approximate models from N network models based on the matching of the data points. At last, we regard these models as the estimation model of the new sensor to calibrate the sensor concentration. This method eliminates the individual differences of a single model to a certain extent and achieves the purpose of rapid calibration. After comparing three kinds of neural networks and conducting relevant experiments, we chose BP neural network as the model, and set the number of selected models to three. The results show that the floating up and down by industry-standard 5% plus or minus 50 ppm calculation, the qualified rate of our method is up to 91.542% between 0 ◦C to 45 ◦C, and the qualified rate even reaches 99.063% between 20 ◦C to 35 ◦C. Compared with similar products, the qualified rate of our method in the calibration of carbon dioxide increases by 12.315% and 22.732% respectively. The algorithm proposed in this paper has a high qualified rate at some temperatures, but the overall qualified rate is relatively lower than it. The main reason is that under high temperature and low temperature conditions, the degree of model adaptation is not high caused by the differences in individual sensor characteristics, which further leads to a low qualified rate of data points. Therefore, in future work, it is necessary to focus on optimizing the adaptation and model construction of the sensor model under high temperature and low temperature conditions, so as to improve the qualified rate of data points at low temperature and high temperature, and further improve the overall performance of the algorithm.
